ANOTHER KEVIN DARCY RATIONALIZATION (I've gotta million of them ... :-)

Okay, imagine that there is a delay between touching the cockatrice, and 
actually turning to stone... How can you save yourself (possible spoilers
ahead)?

	1. Eat a lizard corpse.

	   Requires 2 free hands to do quickly. You don't have 2 free hands,
	   since one of them is instantly turned to stone, and is unuseable.

	2. Polymorph into a "new [wo]man" or into a stone-resistant creature.

	   Negligible chance of this happening randomly. To do deliberately
	   requires 2 free hands. See above.

	3. Pray to your ghod to neutralize petrification

	   Requires 2 free hands to clasp together in prayer gesture. See
	   above.

So, applying the UI suggested above, we don't bother the user with half-a-dozen
messages - we just say

You turn to stone...

and that's that.

Jan> The player has not played the game since, preferring, in his words, to
Jan> indulge in games of skill rather than games of chance. I think this is
Jan> somewhat extreme, but I understand his reasoning. Is NetHack a game of
Jan> skill? If so, will NetHack 3.1 have less of a dependency on random numbers
Jan> and a better user interface?

NetHack is very much a game of skill, IMHO. But, there is a secondary "skill"
in playing/maximizing the odds, which is a broader type of skill from merely 
picking the winningest strategy, and "overcoming" the game, and which, to me,
adds a whole other dimension to my enjoyment. To use an analogy, it's more 
like Bridge than Chess. 

I think your friend saw what he believed to be a "hole" in NetHack's defenses, 
i.e. the wield-a-cockatrice trick, and carefully decided to exploit it to the 
max. All he managed to do, however, was elevate himself to a higher-risk 
bracket, and was derailed by a bad turn of luck and one of NetHack's less-
subtle self-defenses against such exploitation. I'm sorry he gave up on 
NetHack altogether, but if he is not going to interpret such games as 
"learning experiences", then perhaps he is better off with some other pursuit. 
There are plenty others to choose from. De gustabis.

NetHack is neither a deterministic "puzzle" game like some of the "interactive 
fiction" dungeon games, nor is it pure mindless hack-and-slash - it lays 
somewhere in between, and, I don't think it should necessarily be dragged 
towards one or other of the extremes.

